---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { getPost } from "../../data/journal";

const post = getPost("every-control")!;
---

<Layout
  title={`${post.title} | EyesRhythm`}
  description={post.description}
  type="article"
  published={post.date}
  image={post.cover}
  imageAlt={post.coverAlt}
>
  <main id="main">
    <header class="banner">
      <img
        src={post.cover}
        alt={post.coverAlt}
        width="1280"
        height="720"
      />
    </header>

    <article class="wrap article">
      <header class="intro">
        <p class="kicker"><span class="kicker__dot"></span>{post.kicker}</p>
        <h1 class="display">{post.title}</h1>
        <p class="lede">{post.description}</p>
        <p class="meta">{post.read} · 4 November 2026</p>
      </header>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          EyesRhythm sits beside your work and watches blinks through the
          webcam. The video stays on this computer. Below is a quick look at
          each control. The numbers in the pictures are from one afternoon.
          Yours will be your own. Set the
          <a href="/journal/camera-setup">camera</a>
          up once so it can see your eyes.
        </p>

        <h2>How your eyes feel</h2>
        <p>
          When you open the app, a blue bar counts down for three minutes,
          then the app starts on its own. If you have more than one camera,
          pick the one in front of you, or <strong>Hybrid</strong> to use
          every desk camera together. Then choose how your eyes feel. That
          choice stays on this computer and sets Today’s plan.
        </p>
        <figure class="shot">
          <img
            src="/images/guide/splash-comfort.jpg"
            alt="The opening screen with five comfort choices. Comfortable is selected, and Continue is at the bottom."
            width="1040"
            height="896"
          />
          <figcaption>Pick one line, then Continue. Enter does the same.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Comfortable</strong> — aim for 15–20 blinks a minute.</li>
          <li><strong>Mild symptoms</strong> — a full blink, and Practice if you want it.</li>
          <li><strong>Moderate discomfort</strong> — look away now and then, and try Practice before a long stretch.</li>
          <li><strong>Marked irritation</strong> — a gentle day. See an eye doctor if the pain stays.</li>
          <li><strong>Prefer not to say</strong> — the everyday aim of 15–20 blinks a minute.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Three notes, then Start</h2>
        <p>
          Three short notes: the camera stays on this computer, this is a
          wellness companion, and the score ahead is the Ocular Rhythm Index,
          from 0 to 100. Press <strong>Start</strong>, or wait for the timer.
        </p>
        <figure class="shot">
          <img
            src="/images/guide/splash-notices.jpg"
            alt="Three notes before start: on-device privacy, a wellness disclaimer, and a short explanation of the Ocular Rhythm Index. A Start button sits at the bottom."
            width="1040"
            height="966"
          />
          <figcaption>Start begins the session. Waiting does too.</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <h2>The window</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>Live</strong> means a face is in view.
          <strong>Live · looking for you</strong> means the camera is still
          waiting. <strong>Connecting…</strong> means the window is still
          reaching the app.
        </p>
        <figure class="shot">
          <img
            src="/images/guide/dashboard.jpg"
            alt="The full EyesRhythm window: Atlas selected, Today’s plan, four score cards, a quote, and the Beep, Visual, and Practice buttons."
            width="1000"
            height="1724"
          />
          <figcaption>The everyday window. Face in the middle, scores below, three buttons at the bottom.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>
          The face is a stand-in. The pill reads <strong>Eyes open</strong>,
          <strong>Eyes closed</strong>, <strong>Off-screen</strong>, or
          <strong>No face</strong>. <strong>Blinks today</strong> counts while
          a face is in view, and starts again after midnight. Six views sit
          under the face. The one you pick is remembered.
        </p>

        <h2>Atlas, Lumen, and Vesper</h2>
        <p>
          Same companion, three faces. The eyes close when a blink is counted.
          Atlas is warm. Lumen is pale. Vesper is blue.
        </p>
        <figure class="shot">
          <img
            src="/images/guide/lumen.jpg"
            alt="Lumen selected, a pale face with the No face pill and the blinks-today count."
            width="1904"
            height="1148"
          />
          <figcaption>Lumen, the pale face.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure class="shot">
          <img
            src="/images/guide/vesper.jpg"
            alt="Vesper selected, a blue face with the six view buttons underneath."
            width="1904"
            height="1148"
          />
          <figcaption>Vesper, the blue face.</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <h2>Eyes found</h2>
        <p>
          When the camera finds your eyes, the pill says
          <strong>Eyes open</strong> and the drawn eyes stay open between blinks.
        </p>
        <figure class="shot">
          <img
            src="/images/guide/eyes-open.jpg"
            alt="Atlas, the dummy face, with both eyes open. The pill says Eyes open, and the blink count is running."
            width="992"
            height="574"
          />
          <figcaption>Atlas with the eyes found. The pill says Eyes open.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>
          <strong>Lens (C)</strong> swaps the face for the camera picture so
          you can check that both eyes are in the frame. Press C again to
          return to Atlas.
        </p>

        <h2>Trace</h2>
        <p>
          The last five minutes of your score. Now is on the left. The red
          dotted line is the alert line, 50 unless you move it. Orange dots
          are blinks. The line pauses when your face leaves or you look away.
        </p>
        <figure class="shot">
          <img
            src="/images/guide/trace.jpg"
            alt="The Trace view, an empty five-minute chart with a red dotted line at 50 and Trace selected."
            width="1904"
            height="1148"
          />
          <figcaption>Trace waiting for a face. The red dots mark the alert line.</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <h2>Poise</h2>
        <p>
          A blue wireframe of your face, and a dial of where you turned your
          head, in slices of 30°. Green is a direction you already used. Red
          is still waiting. The pulsing slice is the next one to try. The
          number in the middle is how balanced the day has been.
          <strong>Reset</strong> clears today’s dial.
        </p>
        <figure class="shot">
          <img
            src="/images/guide/poise-face.jpg"
            alt="Poise with a blue 3D wireframe face on the left and a dial on the right. A green slice points left, the balance reads 14 percent, and Reset sits under the dial."
            width="992"
            height="772"
          />
          <figcaption>The wireframe beside the dial. This day looked mostly left.</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <h2>Less and Settings</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>Less (D)</strong> hides the scores and shrinks the window to
          the face and the three bottom buttons. The button then reads
          <strong>More</strong>. <strong>Settings (W)</strong> opens a side
          panel with Settings, Reports, and About.
        </p>
        <figure class="shot">
          <img
            src="/images/guide/compact.jpg"
            alt="The compact window: a note that the camera cannot see a face yet, Atlas, More, Settings, and the three bottom buttons."
            width="1000"
            height="1022"
          />
          <figcaption>Less hides the cards. More brings them back.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>
          If no face shows up, a pale note asks you to open Lens or change the camera.
        </p>

        <h2>Today’s plan and the four cards</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>Today’s plan</strong> repeats the comfort choice in one sentence.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Ocular Rhythm Index</strong> — the main score, 0 to 100. Higher is steadier. See <a href="/journal/count-is-not-rhythm">why the blink count is not enough</a>.</li>
          <li><strong>Screen time</strong> — how long a face has been visible today. Looking away pauses it.</li>
          <li><strong>FPS</strong> — how fast the camera is running.</li>
          <li><strong>Look angle</strong> — how far your face is turned. <strong>Off-screen</strong> means blinks pause.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>The quote card</h2>
        <p>
          A grey card with a one-line tip for each bottom button, then a short
          quote that changes about every half minute.
        </p>

        <h2>Beep, Visual, and Practice</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Beep (B)</strong> — a short sound on every blink.</li>
          <li><strong>Visual (A)</strong> — a soft orange glow when the score stays low. Click again for <strong>Sound</strong>, a short tone at most once a minute.</li>
          <li><strong>Practice (P)</strong> — a two-minute blink coach.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Settings</h2>
        <p>
          Each slider takes effect right away.
          <strong>Restore defaults</strong> puts them back. Your camera choice stays.
        </p>
        <figure class="shot shot--wide">
          <img
            src="/images/guide/settings.jpg"
            alt="Settings open beside the window: camera menu, ORI threshold, poor-rhythm minutes, writing angle, turn angle, blink sensitivity, and a list of recent changes."
            width="2000"
            height="2272"
          />
          <figcaption>The sliders. Turn angle and blink sensitivity have already been moved in this picture.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Camera</strong> — pick a webcam, or Hybrid when more than one is connected. Changing it restarts the camera.</li>
          <li><strong>ORI threshold</strong> — 20 to 80, starts at 50. Scores under this line count as low. Trace’s red line moves with it.</li>
          <li><strong>Poor-rhythm minutes</strong> — 1 to 5, starts at 3. How long a low score lasts before the glow or the sound.</li>
          <li><strong>Writing angle</strong> — 10° to 40°, starts at 20°. How far down still counts as the screen.</li>
          <li><strong>Turn angle</strong> — 20° to 80°, starts at 50°. How far left or right still counts as the screen.</li>
          <li><strong>Blink sensitivity</strong> — 0.10 to 0.30, starts at 0.18. How far the lids must close before a blink counts. Lower catches a smaller blink.</li>
          <li><strong>Recent changes</strong> — a short local log of what you moved.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Reports</h2>
        <p>
          History for <strong>Daily</strong>, <strong>Weekly</strong>, or
          <strong>Monthly</strong>. Arrows move the period. The date opens a
          calendar. <strong>Today</strong> jumps back. The green line is the
          target at 75. The red line is the alert line.
        </p>
        <figure class="shot shot--wide">
          <img
            src="/images/guide/reports.jpg"
            alt="The Reports tab on Daily, with an ocular rhythm chart across 24 hours and a screen-time chart below it."
            width="2000"
            height="1880"
          />
          <figcaption>Daily. Green is the target at 75. Red is the alert line at 50.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure class="shot shot--wide">
          <img
            src="/images/guide/reports-calendar.jpg"
            alt="The Reports calendar open on November 2026, with the 4th selected and dots on days that have records."
            width="2000"
            height="2388"
          />
          <figcaption>Tap the date to open the calendar.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>
          <strong>Ocular rhythm</strong> is the score: five-minute slices on
          Daily, one bar a day on Weekly and Monthly.
          <strong>Screen time</strong> is minutes on Daily, and hours on the
          longer views.
        </p>
        <figure class="shot shot--wide">
          <img
            src="/images/guide/reports-weekly.jpg"
            alt="Weekly reports for 2 to 8 November 2026, with Monday, Tuesday, and Wednesday filled in."
            width="2000"
            height="1880"
          />
          <figcaption>Weekly. Empty days stay blank.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure class="shot shot--wide">
          <img
            src="/images/guide/reports-monthly.jpg"
            alt="Monthly reports for November 2026, with bars on the first few days of the month."
            width="2000"
            height="1880"
          />
          <figcaption>Monthly. The arrows move one month at a time.</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <h2>About</h2>
        <p>
          The product name, the version, a short wellness note, and
          <a href="mailto:support@eyesrhythm.com">support@eyesrhythm.com</a>.
          Notices lists the open-source licenses.
        </p>
        <figure class="shot shot--wide">
          <img
            src="/images/guide/about.jpg"
            alt="The About tab, with product, version 1.5.0, description, disclaimer, support email, and notices."
            width="2000"
            height="1880"
          />
          <figcaption>About. This copy is version 1.5.0.</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <h2>Practice</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>Practice (P)</strong> counts down for about 20 seconds, then
          runs for two minutes. Follow the ball, blink on each beep, and keep
          your head still. <strong>Esc</strong> or End practice leaves early.
        </p>
        <figure class="shot shot--wide">
          <img
            src="/images/guide/practice.jpg"
            alt="The Practice screen counting down from 19 seconds, with End practice and the Esc hint at the top."
            width="1800"
            height="1280"
          />
          <figcaption>The 20-second wait. Esc leaves at any time.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>
          At the end, a small card shows the blink rate and the regularity
          from those two minutes.
        </p>

        <h2>Keys</h2>
        <p>These work while the window is focused.</p>
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Key</th>
              <th>What it does</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>B</td>
              <td>Beep on each counted blink, on or off</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>A</td>
              <td>Switch the alert between Visual and Sound</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>P</td>
              <td>Start Practice</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Esc</td>
              <td>End Practice</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>D</td>
              <td>Less or More</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>W</td>
              <td>Open or hide Settings</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>C</td>
              <td>Lens, or back to Atlas</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Enter</td>
              <td>Continue or Start, on the opening screens</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <h2>Then leave it</h2>
        <p>
          Once the camera can see you, leave the window on Less. Open Settings
          for a slider, Reports for the day, and Practice for two minutes of blinks.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="back">
        <a href="/journal">All journal</a>
        ·
        <a href="/blink-rate-detector">Blink rate detector</a>
        ·
        <a href="/journal/camera-setup">Set up your camera so blinks count</a>
        ·
        <a href="/journal/count-is-not-rhythm">Why the blink count is not enough</a>
      </p>
    </article>
  </main>
</Layout>

<style>
  .banner {
    width: min(var(--wrap), calc(100% - var(--gutter) * 2));
    margin: 28px auto 0;
  }

  .banner img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    border-radius: 28px;
  }

  .intro {
    display: grid;
    gap: 12px;
    max-width: 42rem;
  }

  .intro .display {
    font-size: clamp(2.2rem, 5vw, 3.4rem);
    max-width: 16ch;
  }

  .meta {
    font-size: 0.88rem;
    color: var(--text-soft);
  }

  .article {
    padding: 48px 0 96px;
    display: grid;
    gap: 16px;
    justify-items: start;
  }

  .shot {
    margin: 8px 0 4px;
    width: min(100%, 42rem);
  }

  .shot--wide {
    width: min(100%, 52rem);
  }

  .shot img {
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    border-radius: 20px;
    background: #e8eef6;
  }

  .shot figcaption {
    margin-top: 8px;
    font-size: 0.88rem;
    color: var(--text-soft);
  }

  .back {
    margin-top: 12px;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
  }
</style>
